. Neither do we know how these forms interacted with pre-existing religions or to what extent they spread beyond urban centres. It is clear, however, that very gradually many local deities became incorporated into the Sanskritic religions, giving these a particular regional flavour. One distinct regional feature is the persisting popularity of powerful female deities: Monosha (manasā), who protects worshippers against snakebites, Chondi (candī), the goddess of forest life and hunting, Shitola (śītalā), who guards against smallpox, and the fierce and vengeful Kali (kāli)."'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 955, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 762})
